#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Create a string stream to hold the HTML output
    std::stringstream htmlStream;

    // Write the Markdown text to the string stream
    htmlStream << markdown;

    // Return the HTML string
    return htmlStream.str();
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Hello Markdown\nThis is a paragraph.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a `std::stringstream` to write the Markdown text to the stream, which automatically converts it to HTML. The `std::stringstream` class is used to hold the HTML output, and the `<<` operator is used to write the Markdown text to the stream. The `str()` method is used to extract the HTML string from the stream.

The `main` function demonstrates how to use the `markdownToHtml` function by converting a sample Markdown text to HTML and printing the results.

